2008-02-20  Havard Eidnes <he@uninett.no>

	* traffic/common/read-5m-sum.pl: Declare global variables using our().

	Use the globals %save_base, %save_lasttime, and %save_varnames to save
	and restore data when the same surrounding script reads data for
	multiple log files.  Use these to set up %lasttime and %base before
	reading the raw data log, to ensure that we don't lose any samples..

	Correctly handle the quantization into 5-minute intervals when data
	from the previous day is available.  Also handle "leftover" data from
	the previous day which didn't completely fill a 5-minute interval, and
	similarly leave the remaining data at EOF in $leftover{$line,$var}, so
	that this script can correctly stitch together data from multiple
	days, and not lose any samples.

	If the surrounding script terminates between subsequent runs (as would
	be the case for a script to update statistics in near-real-time), it
	ought to save and restore the %leftover array, and also the
	%save_base, %save_lasttime, and %save_varnames arrays mentioned above.

	* traffic/common/read-raw-log.pl:
	Point out that time stamps are a relative offset from 00:00 local time.
	Export a few more variables: %firsttime, %lasttime, and %dstcomp, the
	latter only for debugging.
	Declare global variables using our().

	Introduce automatic compensation for daylight savings time, so that we
	preserve a monotonically increasing time relative to 00:00 local time.
	This makes it possible to convert the time stamps to UTC without
	"hiccups" when entering or leaving daylight savings time.
	This only handles daylight savings time changes with 1 hour.
	This means that one day per year will appear to have 23 hours, while
	another will appear to have 25.

